steps.body:
A slice holds its elements in order, so the first way to visit them is the counted loop you already know. Start `i` at 0 and stop before `len(s)`, because the last index is `len(s)-1`.

```go harness
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	s := []int{4200, 8100, 6500, 9900, 3000}
	{{code}}
	fmt.Println("total:", total)
}
```

```go
total := 0
for i := 0; i < len(s); i++ {
	total += s[i]
}
```

Reading `s[len(s)]` is out of range and panics, so the condition is `<`, never `<=`.

For a maximum or minimum, start from the first element, then let each later element replace it when it is bigger or smaller. Starting from 0 would be wrong for a minimum, and `s[0]` panics on an empty slice, so check `len(s) > 0` first when the data may be empty.

When you want to stop as soon as you find something, a while-style loop reads well. You manage `i` yourself and `break` out when the condition is met:

```go harness
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	s := []int{4200, 8100, 6500, 9900, 3000}
	{{code}}
	fmt.Println("first index over 8000:", i)
}
```

```go
i := 0
for i < len(s) {
	if s[i] > 8000 {
		break
	}
	i++
}
```

Use a sentinel such as `-1` for the not-found case.

example.code:
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	steps := []int{4200, 8100, 6500, 9900, 3000}

	total := 0
	maxSteps := steps[0]
	for i := 0; i < len(steps); i++ {
		total += steps[i]
		if steps[i] > maxSteps {
			maxSteps = steps[i]
		}
	}
	fmt.Println("Total:", total)
	fmt.Println("Max:", maxSteps)

	first := -1
	i := 0
	for i < len(steps) {
		if steps[i] > 8000 {
			first = i
			break
		}
		i++
	}
	fmt.Println("First day over 8000:", first)
}

