package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	expenses := []int{12, 40, 7, 25, 3}

	total := 0
	minCost := expenses[0]
	for i := 0; i < len(expenses); i++ {
		total += expenses[i]
		if expenses[i] < minCost {
			minCost = expenses[i]
		}
	}
	fmt.Println("Total:", total)
	fmt.Println("Min:", minCost)

	first := -1
	i := 0
	for i < len(expenses) {
		if expenses[i] > 30 {
			first = i
			break
		}
		i++
	}
	fmt.Println("First expense over 30:", first)
}

steps.body:
Counting indexes by hand is common enough that Go has a shorter form. `for range` hands you the index and the element on every pass:

```go harness
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	fruits := []string{"apple", "banana", "cherry"}
	{{code}}
}
```

```go
for i, f := range fruits {
	fmt.Println(i, f)
}
```

`i` starts at 0, and `f` holds the value of `fruits[i]`. There is no condition to get wrong, so you cannot run off the end.

Go refuses unused variables, so if you only need the elements, put the blank identifier `_` where the index goes:

```go harness
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	prices := [4]int{120, 80, 45, 99}
	sum := 0
	{{code}}
	fmt.Println("sum:", sum)
}
```

```go
for _, p := range prices {
	sum += p
}
```

`range` works the same on arrays and slices. The length of the slice is read once, before the first pass.

Indexes start at 0, so print `i+1` when people expect a list that starts at 1. To average, convert both the total and the count with `float64(...)` before dividing, or the int division drops the decimals.

example.code:
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
	fruits := []string{"apple", "banana", "cherry"}
	for i, f := range fruits {
		fmt.Printf("%d. %s\n", i+1, f)
	}

	prices := [4]int{120, 80, 45, 99}
	sum := 0
	for _, p := range prices {
		sum += p
	}
	fmt.Println("Sum:", sum)
	fmt.Printf("Average: %.2f\n", float64(sum)/float64(len(prices)))
}

